ck":"Correct! The sample was random and all four expected counts (414.7, 225.3, 233.3, 126.7) are far above 5."},{"correct":false,"text":"No—some cells have observed counts below 100","feedback":"The condition involves the EXPECTED counts, and all of them exceed 5 comfortably."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The sample of 1,000 students was random. Check the expected counts.","explanation":""},"key":"l8cn5oBYo9"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-612a97c1bdda77747487ee7393cbf45b.mjs","model":{"question":"The chi-square statistic for the piercing data is about 495. Compared to the drunk driving example (χ² = 1.62), what does this indicate?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"The piercing data provide vastly stronger evidence against independence","feedback":"Correct! A chi-square statistic of 495 indicates the observed counts are enormously far from what independence predicts—overwhelming evidence of a relationship between gender and piercing."},{"correct":false,"text":"The piercing data provide weaker evidence against independence","feedback":"It's the opposite—larger chi-square values mean MORE evidence against independence."},{"correct":false,"text":"The two statistics cannot be meaningfully compared","feedback":"The two statistics are comparable in spirit: both measure discrepancy from independence, and 495 is vastly larger than 1.62."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The chi-square statistic measures the total standardized discrepancy between observed and expected counts.","explanation":""},"key":"T0ZNFpFfXz"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Comment","position":{"start":{"line":69,"column":1},"end":{"line":69,"column":1}},"key":"coQ3xRsYkm"}],"key":"d3zpMB7ngQ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":72,"column":1},"end":{"line":72,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once the chi-square statistic has been calculated, we can get a feel for its size: is there a relatively large difference between what we observed and what the null hypothesis claims, or a relatively small one? It turns out that for a 2-by-2 case like ours, we are inclined to call the chi-square statistic “large” if it is larger than 3.84. Therefore, our drunk driving test statistic (1.62) is not large, indicating that the data are not different enough from the null hypothesis for us to reject it (we will also see this in the p-value not being small). For other cases (other than 2-by-2) there are different cutoffs for what is considered large, which are determined by the null distribution in that case. We are therefore going to rely only on the p-value to draw our conclusions. Even though we cannot always assess the chi-square statistic directly, it was important to learn about it, since it encompasses the idea behind the test.","position":{"start":{"line":72,"column":1},"end":{"line":72,"column":1}},"key":"bQZyCOkDRy"}],"key":"pJ9HIWYyzf"}],"class":"important","key":"OaY3U7P2iU"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":75,"column":1},"end":{"line":75,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: Conditions for the Chi-Square Test","position":{"start":{"line":75,"column":1},"end":{"line":75,"column":1}},"key":"A71nFKgSPE"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-conditions-for-the-chi-square-test","label":"Check Your Understanding: Conditions for the Chi-Square Test","html_id":"check-your-understanding-conditions-for-the-chi-square-test","implicit":true,"key":"NR98s0CkEl"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":77,"column":1},"end":{"line":77,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The purpose of this activity is to continue exploring whether the risk of alcohol problems among New York firefighters and first responders is related to participation in the 9/11 rescue. Here again are the observed counts:","position":{"start":{"line":77,"column":1},"end":{"line":77,"column":1}},"key":"bUcdAhiNve"}],"key":"cPtOUePd